Ladybird refuses an encoded “/” in an extension resource path, as Blink
does in ExtensionURLToRelativeFilePath(), which decodes the path with
UnescapeBinaryURLComponentSafe() and fail_on_path_separators set.
Gecko instead decodes escaped dots and slashes before the URL is
normalized, in SubstitutingProtocolHandler::NewURI(), and then its
ResolveURI() rejects a decoded backslash.

Ladybird checks the real location of the file it opened against
the extension directory. The other engines check by path: Blink’s
ExtensionResource::GetFilePath() requires the normalized resource
to be under the normalized root, Gecko’s ExtensionProtocolHandler::
NewStream() checks nsIFile::Contains(), and WebKit’s WebExtension::
resourceFileURLForPath() compares real paths. Checking the opened
file rather than its path means a symlink swapped in after the
check can’t escape either.
